### A Model-Guided Analysis of Client-Side DNS RR Handling
- **Presenter:** Rahel Fainchtein
- **Slides:** [A Model-Guided Analysis of Client-Side DNS RR Handling](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ufmrg-a-model-guided-analysis-of-client-side-dns-rr-handling-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Rahel Fainchtein presented a formal state-transition framework built in Maude to test client-side (web browser) handling of modern DNS resource records (HTTPS RRs, Encrypted Client Hello [ECH], and Happy Eyeballs v3).
  - Client-side specifications suffer from vagueness, optional behaviors (`SHOULD`/`MAY`), and under-specified edge cases compared to resolver/authoritative specs.
  - Using bounded path exploration and test case minimization, the framework generated 5,000+ test cases to evaluate browser engines (Chromium/Chrome/Edge/Brave, Firefox, and WebKit/Safari/iOS).
  - Divergent behaviors were categorized into direct RFC violations, implementation choices leading to connection failures, and unsupported features:
    - *Chromium TCP Fallback Termination:* When parallel A, AAAA, and HTTPS queries are issued, Chromium terminates pending DNS-over-TCP fallback queries for truncated HTTPS RRs as soon as A/AAAA responses arrive, ignoring valid HTTPS/ECH parameters.
    - *Firefox RFC Violation:* Firefox accepted and processed HTTPS RRs containing the `no-default-alpn` flag without specifying mandatory ALPN values, violating RFC requirements to reject self-inconsistent RRs.
    - *WebKit Uniformity:* WebKit-based browsers exhibit uniform behavior across OS platforms, but dictate behavior for all iOS browser engines.
  - Ongoing work includes expanding formal models to cross-protocol interactions (DNS, TCP, TLS) and automating RFC rule extractions using AI/LLM agents.

- **Questions and Comments:**
  - *Felix Linker:* Asked whether client developers or server operators bear responsibility for handling implementation divergences, and whether generated test cases would be publicly released. Rahel answered that client implementations need improvement, though server operators must remain aware of client fallbacks. Test cases will be published as a open test vector repository.
  - *Jonathan Hoyland:* Asked how ambiguous RFC language (e.g., "may check DNS") is formalized. Rahel noted it requires subjective modeling informed by direct consultation with RFC authors (e.g., RFC 9460 author Ben Schwartz).

